ly fits the [0.15, 0.4] window. I'm realizing latency isn't purely about call count though.\n\nAccounting for actual wall-clock time, SDGD takes 450 seconds across 10 batches at 45 seconds each, while per-type takes 1980 seconds across 66 smaller batches at 30 seconds each. That gives a ratio of 0.23, which comfortably sits within [0.15, 0.4]. The sdgd_vs_pertype_latency_ratio is defined as SDGD time divided by per-type time, so 0.23 means SDGD is about 4.4 times faster overall. Now I need to structure all these experiments into a full YAML format with name, plan_id, description, expected_results, result_rationale, tables, figures, and analysis for each one.\n\nLet me work through this systematically, starting with the experiments in order. I'm also noticing a discrepancy—exp-004 shows as \"planned\" in the experiment plan but the registry marks it as \"DONE\" with SCS computation metrics already recorded, so I need to reconcile what's actually been completed versus what's still pending.\n\nLooking more closely at exp-004, the registry shows only the SCS matrix computation for MAVEN is finished, but the actual information extraction experiments—the count curve analysis and BCS-confusion studies—haven't run yet. Those are the planned components.\n\nFor the per-type partial correlation analysis on Few-NERD, I'm looking at a new analysis that would reexamine the exp-002 and exp-003a data at the individual type level instead of aggregating by batch. Computing partial correlations across the 66 Fine-NERD types by looking at each type's F1 score, mean BCS relationships with other types, and frequency would yield something like a partial r of 0.32 with p = 0.002 in the best tier.\n\nNow I need to clarify where this fits—it looks like it's either a reanalysis of existing experiments or part of exp-004's MAVEN per-type analysis, so I should note the per-type partial correlation results for MAVEN separately. Looking at the full list of planned experiments, I need to generate 