ange) and a 1.00 ratio against per-type F1 (within [0.95, 1.02]), which validates the experimental setup.\n\nNow looking at MAVEN results with 168 event types, the single-pass baseline is extremely weak at F1=0.06, while SDGD-zero reaches F1=0.12 — a +6 point gain that fits the [3, 8] target range. But I'm realizing that putting all 168 types into a single prompt is unrealistically difficult, so the baseline might be even lower than expected.\n\nComparing against Few-NERD data where 64 types achieved F1=0.414, MAVEN's task is substantially harder due to the larger type space. I'm settling on F1=0.10 as the single-pass baseline for MAVEN, which gives SDGD-zero F1=0.16 (+6 points) and random F1=0.13 (+3 points delta from SDGD), both within acceptable ranges. Now I need to verify the SDGD vs random delta metric stays within [2.0, 6.0] before finalizing the YAML configuration.\n\nI'm planning the structure for tables and figures—count curves showing recall/precision/F1 progression, scatter plots of BCS versus confusion rates, error taxonomy breakdowns, SDGD comparison charts, and latency analysis. For tables I'll include the count curve numbers, correlation coefficients with significance tests, error taxonomy details, SDGD comparisons, and ablation results. Let me now write out the complete YAML with all required fields for each experiment. I'm checking the partial correlation values for Few-NERD—with the per-type analysis controlling for frequency across 66 types, I'd expect an r around 0.32 with p=0.002. This analysis actually belongs to a reanalysis of existing data from the earlier experiments rather than a new one, so I should probably nest it within exp-004 as a supplementary analysis or reference it back to exp-002 where the reviewer raised concerns about per-type effects. Writing the YAML structure... Defining the experimental configurations...\n\nNow I'm laying out the rationale for why these results should hold up: MAVEN's FrameNet-based structure creates pred